## Proof

**Claim.** Yes. There is a canonical $\mathcal{O}_X$-bilinear homomorphism $[,]:ad(E_G)\times ad(E_G)\to ad(E_G)$ equipping the sheaf $ad(E_G)$ with a Lie algebra structure.

**Step 1: The Lie bracket on $\mathfrak{g}$ is $G$-equivariant for the adjoint action.**

For every $g\in G$ and all $x,y\in\mathfrak{g}$, we have
$$[\operatorname{Ad}_g x,\operatorname{Ad}_g y]=\operatorname{Ad}_g[x,y].$$
Indeed, $\operatorname{Ad}_g = (d\,\operatorname{Int}_g)_e$ where $\operatorname{Int}_g:G\to G$, $h\mapsto ghg^{-1}$, is an automorphism of the Lie group $G$. Its differential at the identity is a Lie algebra automorphism, hence preserves the bracket. Equivalently, $\operatorname{ad}(\operatorname{Ad}_g x)\circ\operatorname{Ad}_g = \operatorname{Ad}_g\circ\operatorname{ad}(x)$, which gives the identity by evaluating on $y$. Thus the bracket
$$[,]:\mathfrak{g}\times\mathfrak{g}\to\mathfrak{g}$$
is a $G$-equivariant bilinear map, where $G$ acts on $\mathfrak{g}\times\mathfrak{g}$ diagonally via $\operatorname{Ad}$.

**Step 2: $G$-equivariant bilinear maps descend to associated bundles.**

Recall $ad(E_G)=E_G\times^G\mathfrak{g}=(E_G\times\mathfrak{g})/\sim$, where $(e\cdot g,\xi)\sim(e,\operatorname{Ad}_g\xi)$. Define
$$[,]_{E}:ad(E_G)\times ad(E_G)\to ad(E_G),\qquad [e,\xi]\,+\,[e,\eta]\;\mapsto\;[e,[\xi,\eta]].$$
We check this is well-defined. If we use the representative $(e\cdot g,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\xi)$ and $(e\cdot g,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\eta)$, the right-hand side becomes
$$[e\cdot g,[\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\xi,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\eta]] = [e\cdot g,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}[\xi,\eta]] = [e,[\xi,\eta]],$$
using Step 1 for the middle equality and the defining relation $\sim$ for the last. Hence the map is independent of the chosen representative.

**Step 3: The descended map is $\mathcal{O}_X$-bilinear.**

The associated bundle $ad(E_G)$ is a holomorphic vector bundle (hence locally free $\mathcal{O}_X$-module). Locally on a trivializing open set $U$ for $E_G$, we have $ad(E_G)|_U\cong\mathcal{O}_U\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathfrak{g}$, and under this identification $[,]_E$ becomes
$$(f\otimes\xi,\;h\otimes\eta)\mapsto fh\otimes[\xi,\eta],$$
which is $\mathcal{O}_U$-bilinear. Bilinearity being local, $[,]_E$ is $\mathcal{O}_X$-bilinear as a morphism of sheaves.

**Step 4: The sheaf Jacobi identity holds.**

Antisymmetry and the Jacobi identity are identities among sections that hold fiberwise (each fiber is $\mathfrak{g}$ with its Lie bracket). Since two sections of a sheaf that agree on every stalk are equal, these identities hold as identities of sheaf morphisms. Concretely, for local sections $s_1,s_2,s_3$ of $ad(E_G)$, the Jacobiator
$$J(s_1,s_2,s_3)=[s_1,[s_2,s_3]]+[s_2,[s_3,s_1]]+[s_3,[s_1,s_2]]$$
vanishes in every stalk (fiberwise Jacobi in $\mathfrak{g}$), hence $J=0$ as a section. Similarly $[s,s]=0$.

**Conclusion.** The $G$-equivariance of the Lie bracket on $\mathfrak{g}$ guarantees that the bracket descends to a well-defined $\mathcal{O}_X$-bilinear morphism $[,]:ad(E_G)\times ad(E_G)\to ad(E_G)$, and the fiberwise Lie algebra identities (antisymmetry, Jacobi) hold as sheaf identities. Therefore $ad(E_G)$ carries a canonical Lie algebra structure as a sheaf of $\mathcal{O}_X$-modules. $\blacksquare$

